# CODEOWNERS routes review requests. Combined with the branch ruleset on main
# (no direct pushes, one approving review, approvals dismissed on new commits),
# it is what makes "reviewed by a teammate" a mechanism rather than a habit.
#
# NOTE: this file is listed in .prettierignore. VS Code detects it as Markdown,
# and Prettier then rewrites the catch-all `*` below into a Markdown bullet
# (`-`), which silently destroys the rule: `- @user` is a pattern matching a
# file named "-". The Prettier CLI skips the file for lack of a parser, so
# format:check reports success either way and CI cannot catch it. Do not remove
# the ignore entry.
#
# Two separate effects, and only the first is on by default:
#   1. routing    GitHub auto-requests review from the owner of every path a PR
#                 touches. Works as soon as the handles are valid.
#   2. gating     with "Require review from Code Owners" enabled in the ruleset,
#                 the PR cannot merge until an OWNER approves, not just anyone.
#
# Rules:
#   - later matches win, so the catch-all comes first;
#   - an owner must be a repository COLLABORATOR WITH WRITE ACCESS or the entry
#     is silently ignored, which reads as "protected" while requesting review
#     from nobody. A pending, unaccepted invitation does not count;
#   - directories need a trailing slash to match their contents;
#   - after any edit, `gh api repos/VictorBro/ft_transcendence/codeowners/errors`
#     reports every owner GitHub could not resolve. It does NOT validate
#     patterns, so it would not notice the Markdown corruption described above.

# --- default -----------------------------------------------------------------
# The whole team owns everything. Deliberately flat: in the early phase all five
# of us touch every area, so per-directory ownership would mostly add waiting
# time without improving review quality. Split it the day PRs start going to the
# wrong person.
*   @VictorBro @avarrett @baschni @luca-mattei @VYAntares

# --- narrow once roles are assigned ------------------------------------------
# The subject requires documented team roles (PO, Scrum Master, Tech Lead,
# Developers). When they are decided, give the paths below named owners: a
# one-line change under any of them can turn every gate green without fixing
# anything, by deleting a workflow step, relaxing a coverage threshold or
# adding a console-gate allowlist entry. Those edits should not merge on a
# distracted approval from someone reviewing a UI change.
#
# List at least TWO owners per path. With "Require review from Code Owners"
# enabled, an author cannot approve their own PR, so a sole owner makes their
# own infrastructure changes unmergeable.
#
# /.github/             @owner-a @owner-b
# /scripts/             @owner-a @owner-b
# /e2e/                 @owner-a @owner-b
# /infra/               @owner-a @owner-b
# /.devcontainer/       @owner-a @owner-b
# /Makefile             @owner-a @owner-b
# /compose.yml          @owner-a @owner-b
# /compose.override.yml @owner-a @owner-b
# /compose.prod.yml     @owner-a @owner-b
# /apps/api/Dockerfile  @owner-a @owner-b
# /apps/web/Dockerfile  @owner-a @owner-b
#
# docs/VERSIONS.md is load-bearing: trap 1 is enforced by CI against it,
# and the pnpm files decide what every package resolves to. Whole-team review,
# so a bump is a decision rather than a drive-by.
# /docs/VERSIONS.md     @VictorBro @avarrett @baschni @luca-mattei @VYAntares
# /pnpm-lock.yaml       @owner-a @owner-b
# /pnpm-workspace.yaml  @owner-a @owner-b
